Dear 3D ADEPT Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. The desktop application adapts imported 3D
models, constructs printable parts from dimensions and tested components, and
prepares them for manufacturing locally. It offers a software story at the
intersection of AM workflow and controlled AI: the language model is an
optional interface to a fixed operation API, not a geometry engine.

Every modification is a registered, parameterised operation evaluated by
deterministic geometry code. The same operation is called from a menu,
shortcut or chat. The LLM cannot execute source code, and executable code is
never embedded in a project file. Reusable custom parts travel as recipes of
registered operations and values, with fields, previews and tested parameter
ranges.

That architecture now supports practical editing workflows. Version 0.3.0
recognises a hole in imported STL/3MF geometry and changes its diameter on the
original axis. Users can add standards-based inserts, screws and bearing
seats, apply fits measured from printed calibration parts, sketch directly in
the 3D view, and carry individual spool process data into 3MF and the slicer.

The release history records 220 curated, visible changes since the first 0.1
demo. Solidon runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without an account, cloud
storage or telemetry. Cloud AI is optional; local models can run through
Ollama, and all non-chat functions remain fully available offline.

Product: https://solidon3d.de/en/
